Version Control & Why Git Matters (Ch. 1)
Version Control is basically the discipline of keeping track of every change in a project. Git records additions, edits, and deletions and lets you roll back whenever needed. The textbook describes it as a way to avoid the chaos of “final_v2_really_final” files, and that’s exactly how I used to work. Git replaces that mess with structured snapshots and descriptions.

There are three major types of VCS:
 * Local — everything stored on one machine; risky and outdated.
 * Centralized — one server; allows teamwork but locks files and creates bottlenecks.
 * Distributed (Git) — every user has a full copy of the repo and history. This solves the “single point of failure” problem and makes branching, merging, and collaboration smooth.

Git was created because BitKeeper stopped being free. Linus Torvalds built Git to be fast, distributed, and reliable. It’s now the industry standard.
Key takeaway: Git isn’t just a tool — it’s a safety net. It lets you experiment without fear because every version is preserved.


Installing & Setting Up Git (Ch. 2)
Git installation is straightforward across Windows, Mac, and Linux. The important part is the setup:

Code
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "you@example.com"

This identity is attached to every commit. The textbook emphasizes that this setup is global, meaning you only do it once per system.
Choosing an editor matters only because Git opens it for commit messages. You can use Nano, Vim, VS Code, etc.
Reflection: I didn’t realize Git needed a default editor. It makes sense now — commit messages are part of the workflow, not an afterthought.



Repositories, Working Directory, Staging, and Commits (Ch. 3)
A Git repository is just a folder with a hidden .git directory inside. That .git folder is the database storing all snapshots.

Git’s workflow revolves around three states:
 * Working Directory — where you edit files
 * Staging Area — where you mark files to be included in the next snapshot
 * Commit — the snapshot itself, stored in .git

Commands:
 * git status — shows what changed
 * git add — moves files to staging
 * git commit — saves the snapshot

The textbook stresses that Git tracks snapshots, not diffs. This explains why switching versions is fast — Git simply loads the saved state.
Reflection: Understanding the three states made Git feel logical instead of mysterious. Staging is like curating what goes into the snapshot, not just blindly saving everything.



Ignoring Files, Logs, and History (Ch. 4)
Not everything belongs in version control. .gitignore lets you exclude files like logs, build outputs, temp files, and private data.

Examples:
 * PRIVATE.txt — ignore a specific file
 * build/ — ignore a directory
 * *.exe — ignore all executables
 * !output.exe — exception to a rule

The textbook reminds that .gitignore itself is tracked, so you must commit it.

History tools:
 * git log — shows commit history
 * git show <commit> — shows changes in a specific commit
 * git diff — shows changes since last commit
 * git diff --staged — shows staged changes

Reflection: Seeing how Git logs everything made me appreciate how transparent the system is. I can see exactly what changed, when, and why — something I never had before.



Navigating, Undoing, and Amending Commits (Ch. 5)
Git allows time travel — safely.

Navigating versions
 * git checkout <commit> — view an older snapshot
 * git checkout master — return to the present
   The textbook warns: never switch commits with unstaged changes.

Undoing commits
 * git revert <commit> — creates a new commit that reverses the old one
   This preserves history instead of rewriting it.

Fixing commits
 * git commit --amend — rewrite the last commit message or include forgotten files
   This replaces the previous commit with a new one.

Resetting (dangerous)
 * git reset HEAD <file> — unstage
 * git reset --soft HEAD~1 — undo last commit but keep changes staged

Reflection: I used to think undoing changes meant deleting work. Git’s approach is smarter — it keeps history intact and adds corrective commits. Amending is great for small mistakes, but the textbook warns not to abuse it. I get why: your commit history should tell the story of your work, not hide it.

